WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.450
OT risk involves actors with different goals, resources, and levels of process knowledge.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.976
Cybercriminals often seek payment, resale value, or access they can monetize.

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:14.915
Ransomware operators may disrupt an industrial organization without intentionally manipulating control systems;

00:00:14.915 --> 00:00:19.853
encrypting business and operational support services can be enough to stop production.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:23.557
Nation-state and state-linked groups may pursue espionage, strategic access,

00:00:23.557 --> 00:00:25.203
disruption capability, or influence.

00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:30.141
Their activity can include long-term collection and preparation rather than immediate impact.

00:00:30.141 --> 00:00:34.256
Defenders should avoid assuming that every incident is highly specialized,

00:00:34.256 --> 00:00:38.783
but critical operators should account for patient adversaries with significant resources.

00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:41.252
Insiders possess legitimate knowledge and access.

00:00:41.252 --> 00:00:43.721
A malicious insider may misuse privileges,

00:00:43.721 --> 00:00:48.248
while a well-intentioned employee can create similar exposure by bypassing procedure,

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.717
reusing credentials, or connecting unauthorized equipment.

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:54.832
Contractors and third parties add another form of trusted access.

00:00:54.832 --> 00:00:56.890
Their security practices, endpoints, accounts,

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.593
and remote connections become part of the organization's risk.

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:05.532
Hacktivists may seek visibility, disruption, or symbolic effect against a company, industry,

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:06.766
or public service.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.412
Their technical capability varies.

00:01:08.412 --> 00:01:11.704
Accidental and negligent users are not hostile actors,

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:17.054
but they belong in the threat landscape because routine mistakes can change configurations,

00:01:17.054 --> 00:01:19.112
weaken boundaries, or interrupt operations.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:22.404
Actor labels should guide preparation, not replace evidence.

00:01:22.404 --> 00:01:26.931
The same remote-access pathway could be misused by a criminal, contractor,

00:01:26.931 --> 00:01:28.577
or compromised employee account.

00:01:28.577 --> 00:01:32.692
Layered controls such as individual identities, least privilege, segmentation, monitoring,

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:36.807
approval, and change records reduce risk across several actor types.

00:01:36.807 --> 00:01:40.511
Focus on what access exists, what it can influence,

00:01:40.511 --> 00:01:42.980
and how misuse would be detected.
